WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.416 --> 00:00:11.383
The Games are a sporting event like no other.

2
00:00:11.803 --> 00:00:17.447
They are the world's largest sporting eventㅡthe Games have more athletes, more sports and more participant countries than

3
00:00:17.547 --> 00:00:19.369
any other sports competition in the world.

4
00:00:20.229 --> 00:00:24.212
In 2004, the Olympic Games are returning to their ancient birthplace in Greece.

5
00:00:25.193 --> 00:00:28.195
The Athens Olympic Games will be held from the 13th to the 29th of August 2004.

6
00:00:30.476 --> 00:00:34.239
The competition schedule includes 28 Olympics sports at 35 venues.

7
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.823
While the ancient Olympics have a long history of over one thousand years, the modern Olympics have a

8
00:00:38.863 --> 00:00:39.844
much shorter history.

9
00:00:41.826 --> 00:00:46.790
In 1894, a French aristocrat named Pierre de Coubertin dreamt of reviving them.

10
00:00:47.931 --> 00:00:52.795
Like the Ancient Greeks, he believed sport had an essential role in the education system, and in the

11
00:00:52.835 --> 00:00:54.436
formation of strong moral character.

12
00:00:55.016 --> 00:01:00.469
He was also a keen sportsman himself, and practiced the sports of boxing, fencing, horse-riding and rowing.

13
00:01:01.149 --> 00:01:05.152
Coubertin decided the best way to promote sport was to revive the Olympic Games.

14
00:01:05.692 --> 00:01:12.117
He formed an International Olympic Committee (IOC), whose job it was to organize the first modern Olympic Games.

15
00:01:12.857 --> 00:01:18.061
They did this successfully and in 1896, the first modern Olympic Games were held in Athens, Greece.

16
00:01:18.841 --> 00:01:22.444
They were held in Athens as a reminder that the Olympic Games originated in Greece.

17
00:01:24.645 --> 00:01:28.808
The Olympic Games in 1896 featured many references to the ancient Games.

18
00:01:29.516 --> 00:01:33.837
The majority of the competitions took place in the ancient stadium, which was restored for the occasion.

19
00:01:34.358 --> 00:01:39.579
Some of the sports from the ancient Games were echoed in the modern games, including weightlifting, wrestling and

20
00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:44.701
athletics. There was also cycling, fencing, gymnastics, swimming, tennis and shooting.

21
00:01:45.141 --> 00:01:49.602
The organizers even went as far as inventing a new race, inspired by a legendary event in Ancient

22
00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:51.423
Greek history: the marathon race.

23
00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:57.261
This race commemorates the exploit of a soldier who, in 490 BC, ran from the town of Marathon

24
00:01:57.281 --> 00:02:00.063
to Athens to announce that the Persians had been defeated in battle.

25
00:02:00.644 --> 00:02:03.686
Legend has it that the soldier delivered the message "Niki!"

26
00:02:03.706 --> 00:02:06.328
("victory"), then collapsed and died.

27
00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:09.111
The distance was approximately 42 km.

28
00:02:10.171 --> 00:02:12.513
The modern Olympic Games have twenty-eight different sports.

29
00:02:12.814 --> 00:02:15.936
The IOC sets the program and decides which sports will be included.

30
00:02:16.477 --> 00:02:24.303
Unlike the ancient Games, individuals represent their country, and team sports like rowing, softball, baseball, basketball, soccer, volleyball

31
00:02:24.543 --> 00:02:25.604
and hockey are included.

32
00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:32.563
Until 1992, lesser known sports were also included as "demonstration sports" alongside the official Olympic program.

33
00:02:33.183 --> 00:02:38.385
Some demonstration sports include bandy (soccer on ice), dogsled racing and water skiing.

34
00:02:39.005 --> 00:02:44.788
The Olympic program has come a long way since 1896: some sports have been discontinued (e.g.

35
00:02:45.088 --> 00:02:50.190
golf, polo and tug-of-war), others were dropped and then reintroduced (e.g.

36
00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:54.992
archery), while several new sports have been added (e.g.

37
00:02:55.012 --> 00:02:56.693
the triathlon and taekwondo).

38
00:02:57.913 --> 00:03:01.994
For both Coubertin and the ancient Greeks, the Olympics were not just about sport.

39
00:03:02.514 --> 00:03:08.276
He believed this sports competition was different because it had principles: to strive for perfection, to represent the

40
00:03:08.316 --> 00:03:13.157
best athletes in the world and for everyone involved to display high moral character and a spirit of

41
00:03:13.237 --> 00:03:13.817
fair play.

42
00:03:14.338 --> 00:03:18.539
The symbols of the Olympic Games reflect the ideals of Coubertin and the Olympic movement.

43
00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:22.740
The most important Olympic symbols are the rings, the motto and the flame.

44
00:03:23.392 --> 00:03:25.113
The rings represent five continents.

45
00:03:25.533 --> 00:03:29.375
They are interlaced to show the meeting of the athletes of the whole world during the Olympic Games.

46
00:03:29.916 --> 00:03:32.637
On the Olympic flag, the rings appear on a white background.

47
00:03:33.017 --> 00:03:36.860
The six colors of the flag, including the white background, represent all nations.

48
00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:44.564
The motto is made up of three Latin words: Citius, Altius, Fortius (Faster, Higher, Stronger).

49
00:03:45.404 --> 00:03:50.207
These three words encourage the athlete to give his or her best during competition, and to view this

50
00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:51.668
effort as a victory in itself.

51
00:03:52.916 --> 00:03:58.220
In memory of the Olympic Games' origins, a flame is lit in Olympia, Greece, some months before the

52
00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.121
opening of the Games.

53
00:03:59.681 --> 00:04:02.023
The Olympic flame can only be lit by the sun's rays.

54
00:04:02.403 --> 00:04:06.326
The flame, burning in the Olympic torch, is carried from Olympia to the host country.

55
00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:12.290
Past and present Olympic athletes, as well as ordinary citizens, run with the torch through towns and cities.

56
00:04:14.331 --> 00:04:17.714
Modern Olympians are as legendary as the ancient Greek heroes before them.

57
00:04:18.174 --> 00:04:18.774
As Anita L.

58
00:04:18.794 --> 00:04:26.375
DeFrantz, a former Olympian, says, "Excellence comes in every shape, size, race and sex Each sport takes a

59
00:04:26.435 --> 00:04:29.397
special skill and determination for a person to ascend to the top."

60
00:04:29.998 --> 00:04:34.721
Soonyung Kim was only 17 years old when she won gold medals in both the individual and team

61
00:04:34.821 --> 00:04:37.143
archery events at the 1988 Seoul Olympics.

62
00:04:37.923 --> 00:04:43.527
In the 1992 Barcelona Olympics, Kim won the silver medal in the individual event and another gold medal

63
00:04:43.607 --> 00:04:44.288
in the team event.

64
00:04:44.688 --> 00:04:46.490
She then retired at the age of 21.

65
00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:49.712
Over the next seven years, Kim married and raised two children.

66
00:04:50.196 --> 00:04:55.058
In 1999, she started training again and qualified for the South Korean Olympic team for the Sydney Games.

67
00:04:55.718 --> 00:04:58.640
In Sydney, she finished third in the individual archery event.

68
00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:03.121
Soonyung Kim now had a complete set of medalsㅡgold, silver and bronze.

69
00:05:04.522 --> 00:05:07.463
One of Korea's most determined Olympic heroes was Keechung Sohn.

70
00:05:08.183 --> 00:05:13.886
As Korea was occupied by Japanese forces, Sohn had to participate in the 1936 Berlin Olympics as a

71
00:05:13.926 --> 00:05:14.906
Japanese team member.

72
00:05:15.506 --> 00:05:18.067
He was forced to use a Japanese name, Kitei Son.

73
00:05:19.008 --> 00:05:22.057
At the Games, Sohn resisted the pressure from the Japanese.

74
00:05:22.437 --> 00:05:26.820
He always signed with his Korean name, and whenever he was asked where he was from, he explained

75
00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.341
that Korea was a separate country.

76
00:05:29.141 --> 00:05:33.424
Sohn became a Korean national hero when he won the Berlin Olympic marathon by more than two minutes.

77
00:05:34.084 --> 00:05:39.667
One newspaper, Dong-a-Ilbo, published a photo of Sohn on the victory platform-but with one change: they painted over

78
00:05:39.687 --> 00:05:41.148
the Japanese flag on his sweatshirt.

79
00:05:41.548 --> 00:05:44.750
Staff members were jailed and the newspaper's publication was suspended.

80
00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.572
Sohn carried the South Korean flag in the Opening Ceremony of the London Olympics.

81
00:05:49.212 --> 00:05:53.535
Forty years later, Keechung Sohn entered the Seoul Olympic Stadium bearing the Olympic torch.

82
00:05:54.015 --> 00:05:57.278
The 76-year-old Sohn ran with pride and the crowd cheered him on.

83
00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:05.023
Like the ancient Olympics, the modern Olympics have been the place for politics as well as sports events.

84
00:06:05.103 --> 00:06:11.547
In 1968 in the Mexico City Olympics, American athletes Tommy Smith and John Carlos demonstrated against racial segregation

85
00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:12.488
in the United States.

86
00:06:12.928 --> 00:06:17.592
When the American flag was raised to celebrate their victory, the athletes raised their fists and bowed their

87
00:06:17.612 --> 00:06:21.010
heads. This gesture showed their support for the rights of African Americans.

88
00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:23.592
As a result, however, they were sent home.

89
00:06:23.672 --> 00:06:27.034
In 1972, Palestinian terrorists took Israeli athletes hostage.

90
00:06:27.395 --> 00:06:32.198
They wanted to bring the world's attention to Palestine's situation, and demand the release of Arab hostages held

91
00:06:32.238 --> 00:06:32.958
by the Israelis.

92
00:06:33.599 --> 00:06:38.182
The event ended in tragedy when the hostages were executed by the terrorists, before in turn they were

93
00:06:38.222 --> 00:06:39.063
killed by the police.

94
00:06:39.963 --> 00:06:45.187
At the opening ceremony of the 2000 games in Sydney, Australia, South Korea and North Korea marched together

95
00:06:45.267 --> 00:06:46.128
under a single flag.

96
00:06:46.508 --> 00:06:48.549
They were greeted by thunderous applause and tears.

97
00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:55.253
When the founder of the modern Olympic Games was asked, "Why did you restore the Olympic Games?"

98
00:06:55.573 --> 00:07:02.978
Pierre de Coubertin replied, "To ennoble and strengthen sports and to ensure their independence and duration The 2004

99
00:07:03.118 --> 00:07:04.518
Athens Olympics starts soon.

100
00:07:05.179 --> 00:07:11.162
During this time, athletes and spectators alike will experience some intense moments: a new record, dignity in defeat

101
00:07:11.242 --> 00:07:14.084
and victory, and the feeling of being part of another Olympiad.
